Set theory is the lingua franca of modern mathematics. Every mathematical object---numbers, functions, spaces, algebras---can be constructed from sets. For the programming language scientist, set theory provides the vocabulary for defining syntax (sets of terms), semantics (sets of states and transitions), and type systems (sets of types and typing judgments).

This chapter develops the set-theoretic foundations needed throughout the book: sets, functions, relations, equivalence relations, partial orders, and fixed-point theorems. We pay particular attention to the Knaster--Tarski theorem, which is the mathematical engine behind abstract interpretation and dataflow analysis.

\section{Sets}\label{sec:set-basics}

\subsection{Na\"ive Set Theory}

We work in na\"ive set theory, which takes the notion of ``set'' as primitive. A set is a collection of objects, called its \emph{elements} or \emph{members}.

\begin{definition}[Set Membership and Extensionality]\label{def:set-membership}
If $a$ is an element of a set $A$, we write $a \in A$. The \emph{axiom of extensionality} states that two sets are equal if and only if they have the same elements:
\[A = B \iff \forall x.\,(x \in A \leftrightarrow x \in B)\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Set Construction]\label{def:set-construction}
Sets can be specified in several ways:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Enumeration}: $A = \{1, 2, 3\}$
  \item \textbf{Set-builder notation}: $A = \{x \in U \mid P(x)\}$ (the set of all $x$ in universe $U$ satisfying property $P$)
  \item \textbf{Recursive definition}: $A$ is the smallest set satisfying certain closure conditions
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Basic Set Operations]\label{def:set-operations}
For sets $A$ and $B$:
\begin{align*}
  A \cup B &= \{x \mid x \in A \text{ or } x \in B\} && \text{(union)} \\
  A \cap B &= \{x \mid x \in A \text{ and } x \in B\} && \text{(intersection)} \\
  A \setminus B &= \{x \mid x \in A \text{ and } x \notin B\} && \text{(difference)} \\
  A \times B &= \{(a, b) \mid a \in A \text{ and } b \in B\} && \text{(Cartesian product)} \\
  \mathcal{P}(A) &= \{S \mid S \subseteq A\} && \text{(powerset)}
\end{align*}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Special Sets]\label{def:special-sets}
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\emptyset$ --- the empty set (the unique set with no elements)
  \item $\{a\}$ --- singleton set
  \item $\mathbb{N} = \{0, 1, 2, \ldots\}$ --- natural numbers
  \item $\mathbb{Z}$ --- integers
  \item $\mathbb{Q}$ --- rationals
  \item $\mathbb{R}$ --- reals
  \item $\mathbb{B} = \{\mathbf{tt}, \mathbf{ff}\}$ --- Boolean values
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\section{Functions}\label{sec:set-functions}

\begin{definition}[Function]\label{def:function}
A \emph{function} $f : A \to B$ from set $A$ (the \emph{domain}) to set $B$ (the \emph{codomain}) is a set of ordered pairs $(a, b) \in A \times B$ such that for every $a \in A$, there is exactly one $b \in B$ with $(a, b) \in f$. We write $f(a) = b$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Injective, Surjective, Bijective]\label{def:injective-surjective}
A function $f : A \to B$ is:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \emph{Injective} (one-to-one) if $f(a_1) = f(a_2) \implies a_1 = a_2$
  \item \emph{Surjective} (onto) if for every $b \in B$, there exists $a \in A$ with $f(a) = b$
  \item \emph{Bijective} if it is both injective and surjective
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Function Composition]\label{def:composition}
Given $f : A \to B$ and $g : B \to C$, the \emph{composition} $g \circ f : A \to C$ is defined by $(g \circ f)(a) = g(f(a))$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Partial Function]\label{def:partial-function}
A \emph{partial function} from $A$ to $B$ is a function from a subset of $A$ to $B$. We write $f : A \rightharpoonup B$. The \emph{domain of definition} of $f$ is $\mathrm{dom}(f) = \{a \in A \mid f(a) \text{ is defined}\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{insight}
Partial functions are ubiquitous in programming language semantics. The semantics of a program may be a partial function from states to states: it is defined only for those initial states from which the program terminates. The study of partial functions leads naturally to domain theory (see \chref{order-theory}).
\end{insight}

\section{Relations}\label{sec:set-relations}

\begin{definition}[Relation]\label{def:relation}
A \emph{binary relation} $R$ on sets $A$ and $B$ is a subset $R \subseteq A \times B$. We write $a\,R\,b$ (or $R(a,b)$) to mean $(a,b) \in R$. A relation on a single set $A$ is a subset $R \subseteq A \times A$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Properties of Relations]\label{def:relation-properties}
A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \emph{Reflexive} if $\forall a \in A.\; a\,R\,a$
  \item \emph{Irreflexive} if $\forall a \in A.\; \neg(a\,R\,a)$
  \item \emph{Symmetric} if $\forall a,b \in A.\; a\,R\,b \implies b\,R\,a$
  \item \emph{Antisymmetric} if $\forall a,b \in A.\; a\,R\,b \land b\,R\,a \implies a = b$
  \item \emph{Transitive} if $\forall a,b,c \in A.\; a\,R\,b \land b\,R\,c \implies a\,R\,c$
  \item \emph{Total} (or \emph{connected}) if $\forall a,b \in A.\; a\,R\,b \lor b\,R\,a$
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Relation Operations]\label{def:relation-operations}
Given relations $R, S$ on $A$:
\begin{align*}
  R^{-1} &= \{(b,a) \mid (a,b) \in R\} && \text{(inverse)} \\
  R \circ S &= \{(a,c) \mid \exists b.\; (a,b) \in S \land (b,c) \in R\} && \text{(composition)} \\
  R^+ &= \bigcup_{n \geq 1} R^n && \text{(transitive closure)} \\
  R^* &= \{(a,a) \mid a \in A\} \cup R^+ && \text{(reflexive-transitive closure)}
\end{align*}
\end{definition}

\section{Equivalence Relations}\label{sec:set-equivalence}

\begin{definition}[Equivalence Relation]\label{def:equivalence}
An \emph{equivalence relation} on a set $A$ is a relation $\sim$ that is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Equivalence Class]\label{def:equivalence-class}
Given an equivalence relation $\sim$ on $A$ and an element $a \in A$, the \emph{equivalence class} of $a$ is:
\[[a]_{\sim} = \{b \in A \mid a \sim b\}\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Quotient Set]\label{def:quotient}
The \emph{quotient set} $A/{\sim}$ is the set of all equivalence classes:
\[A/{\sim} = \{[a]_{\sim} \mid a \in A\}\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Partition Theorem]\label{thm:partition}
The equivalence classes of an equivalence relation $\sim$ on $A$ form a \emph{partition} of $A$: they are non-empty, pairwise disjoint, and their union is $A$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $[a]$ and $[b]$ be two equivalence classes. If they share an element $c$, then $a \sim c$ and $b \sim c$. By symmetry, $c \sim b$, and by transitivity, $a \sim b$. Hence for any $x \in [a]$, we have $x \sim a \sim b$, so $x \in [b]$. Thus $[a] \subseteq [b]$, and symmetrically $[b] \subseteq [a]$. So equivalence classes are either identical or disjoint. Each class $[a]$ is non-empty since $a \in [a]$ by reflexivity. The union is $A$ since every $a$ belongs to $[a]$.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}[Alpha-Equivalence]\label{ex:alpha-equivalence}
In the lambda calculus, $\alpha$-equivalence is the equivalence relation that identifies terms differing only in the names of bound variables. For example, $\lambda x.\,x$ and $\lambda y.\,y$ are $\alpha$-equivalent. The quotient set of lambda terms modulo $\alpha$-equivalence is the set of `` genuine'' lambda terms, where variable names are irrelevant.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Type Equivalence]\label{ex:type-equivalence}
In type systems, type equivalence is an equivalence relation on type expressions. For instance, $\alpha \to \beta$ and $\gamma \to \delta$ are equivalent if $\alpha = \gamma$ and $\beta = \delta$. In systems with subtyping, we may also consider the equivalence relation induced by mutual subtyping: $A \sim B$ iff $A <: B$ and $B <: A$.
\end{example}

\section{Partial Orders and Total Orders}\label{sec:set-orders}

\begin{definition}[Partial Order]\label{def:partial-order}
A \emph{partial order} on a set $A$ is a relation $\sqsubseteq$ that is reflexive, antisymmetric, and transitive. The pair $(A, \sqsubseteq)$ is called a \emph{partially ordered set} (or \emph{poset}).
\end{definition}

We write $a \sqsubset b$ for $a \sqsubseteq b \land a \neq b$ (strict order), and $a \sqsupseteq b$ for $b \sqsubseteq a$.

\begin{definition}[Total Order]\label{def:total-order}
A partial order $\sqsubseteq$ on $A$ is a \emph{total order} (or \emph{linear order}) if for all $a, b \in A$, either $a \sqsubseteq b$ or $b \sqsubseteq a$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Bounds and Extrema]\label{def:bounds}
Let $(A, \sqsubseteq)$ be a poset and $S \subseteq A$.
\begin{itemize}
  \item An \emph{upper bound} of $S$ is an element $u \in A$ such that $s \sqsubseteq u$ for all $s \in S$.
  \item A \emph{least upper bound} (supremum, join) of $S$ is an upper bound $u$ such that $u \sqsubseteq v$ for every upper bound $v$ of $S$. Written $\bigsqcup S$ or $\sup S$.
  \item A \emph{lower bound} of $S$ is an element $l \in A$ such that $l \sqsubseteq s$ for all $s \in S$.
  \item A \emph{greatest lower bound} (infimum, meet) of $S$ is a lower bound $l$ such that $k \sqsubseteq l$ for every lower bound $k$ of $S$. Written $\bigsqcap S$ or $\inf S$.
  \item A \emph{least element} (bottom) of $A$ is an element $\bot$ such that $\bot \sqsubseteq a$ for all $a \in A$.
  \item A \emph{greatest element} (top) of $A$ is an element $\top$ such that $a \sqsubseteq \top$ for all $a \in A$.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Hasse Diagram]\label{def:hasse}
The \emph{Hasse diagram} of a finite poset $(A, \sqsubseteq)$ is a graph where:
\begin{itemize}
  \item The vertices are the elements of $A$
  \item There is an edge from $a$ to $b$ (drawn with $b$ above $a$) if $a \sqsubset b$ and there is no $c$ with $a \sqsubset c \sqsubset b$ (i.e., $b$ \emph{covers} $a$)
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Subset Ordering]\label{ex:subset-order}
For any set $S$, the powerset $\mathcal{P}(S)$ ordered by inclusion $(\subseteq)$ is a poset. The bottom element is $\emptyset$, the top element is $S$, the join of $A$ and $B$ is $A \cup B$, and the meet is $A \cap B$.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Divisibility Ordering]\label{ex:divisibility}
The positive integers $\mathbb{Z}^+$ ordered by divisibility ($a \mid b$ iff $b = ka$ for some $k$) form a poset. The bottom element is 1. The join of $a$ and $b$ is $\mathrm{lcm}(a,b)$, and the meet is $\mathrm{gcd}(a,b)$.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Prefix Ordering on Strings]\label{ex:prefix-order}
The set of strings over an alphabet $\Sigma$ ordered by the prefix relation ($u \sqsubseteq v$ iff $v = uw$ for some $w$) is a poset. The bottom element is the empty string $\varepsilon$.
\end{example}

\section{Monotone Functions and Fixed Points}\label{sec:set-fixed-points}

\begin{definition}[Monotone Function]\label{def:monotone}
Let $(A, \sqsubseteq_A)$ and $(B, \sqsubseteq_B)$ be posets. A function $f : A \to B$ is \emph{monotone} (or \emph{order-preserving}) if $a_1 \sqsubseteq_A a_2 \implies f(a_1) \sqsubseteq_B f(a_2)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Fixed Point]\label{def:fixed-point}
A \emph{fixed point} of a function $f : A \to A$ is an element $a \in A$ such that $f(a) = a$. The \emph{least fixed point} (lfp) of $f$, written $\mathrm{lfp}(f)$, is the least element of the set of fixed points.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Complete Lattice]\label{def:complete-lattice}
A poset $(L, \sqsubseteq)$ is a \emph{complete lattice} if every subset $S \subseteq L$ has both a supremum and an infimum.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Knaster--Tarski Fixed-Point Theorem]\label{thm:knaster-tarski}
Let $(L, \sqsubseteq)$ be a complete lattice and $f : L \to L$ a monotone function. Then $f$ has a least fixed point, and it is given by:
\[\mathrm{lfp}(f) = \bigsqcap \{x \in L \mid f(x) \sqsubseteq x\}\]
Moreover, the set of fixed points of $f$ forms a complete lattice.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $P = \{x \in L \mid f(x) \sqsubseteq x\}$ (the set of \emph{pre-fixed points}). Let $a = \bigsqcap P$. We show $f(a) = a$.

Since $a \sqsubseteq x$ for all $x \in P$, and $f$ is monotone, $f(a) \sqsubseteq f(x)$ for all $x \in P$. Since $f(x) \sqsubseteq x$ for all $x \in P$, we have $f(a) \sqsubseteq x$ for all $x \in P$. Thus $f(a)$ is a lower bound of $P$, so $f(a) \sqsubseteq \bigsqcap P = a$.

Since $f(a) \sqsubseteq a$, by monotonicity $f(f(a)) \sqsubseteq f(a)$, so $f(a) \in P$. Therefore $a = \bigsqcap P \sqsubseteq f(a)$.

Combining, $f(a) = a$. Since $a$ is the greatest lower bound of all pre-fixed points, and every fixed point is a pre-fixed point, $a$ is the least fixed point.
\end{proof}

\begin{insight}
The Knaster--Tarski theorem is the mathematical engine behind dataflow analysis and abstract interpretation. In dataflow analysis, we compute properties of programs by finding fixed points of transfer functions on a lattice. The theorem guarantees that these fixed points exist and are unique (as least fixed points), provided the lattice is complete and the transfer functions are monotone.
\end{insight}

\begin{definition}[Iterative Fixed-Point Computation]\label{def:iterative-fp}
For a complete lattice $(L, \sqsubseteq)$ with bottom element $\bot$ and a monotone function $f : L \to L$, the \emph{Kleene chain} is:
\[\bot \sqsubseteq f(\bot) \sqsubseteq f^2(\bot) \sqsubseteq f^3(\bot) \sqsubseteq \cdots\]
If $L$ satisfies the \emph{ascending chain condition} (every ascending chain stabilizes), then $\mathrm{lfp}(f) = f^n(\bot)$ for some finite $n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Kleene's Fixed-Point Theorem]\label{thm:kleene}
Let $(L, \sqsubseteq)$ be a complete lattice and $f : L \to L$ a monotone function. Then:
\[\mathrm{lfp}(f) = \bigsqcup_{n \geq 0} f^n(\bot)\]
\end{theorem}

\section{Cardinality and Countability}\label{sec:set-cardinality}

\begin{definition}[Cardinality]\label{def:cardinality}
Two sets $A$ and $B$ have the same \emph{cardinality}, written $|A| = |B|$, if there exists a bijection between them. We write $|A| \leq |B|$ if there exists an injection from $A$ to $B$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Countable and Uncountable]\label{def:countable}
A set $A$ is:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \emph{Finite} if $|A| = n$ for some $n \in \mathbb{N}$
  \item \emph{Countably infinite} (or \emph{denumerable}) if $|A| = |\mathbb{N}|$
  \item \emph{Countable} if it is finite or countably infinite
  \item \emph{Uncountable} if it is not countable
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Cantor's Theorem]\label{thm:cantor}
For any set $A$, $|A| < |\mathcal{P}(A)|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Suppose $f : A \to \mathcal{P}(A)$ is a bijection. Let $D = \{a \in A \mid a \notin f(a)\}$. Since $f$ is surjective, there exists $d \in A$ with $f(d) = D$. If $d \in D$, then $d \notin f(d) = D$, contradiction. If $d \notin D$, then $d \in f(d) = D$, contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Uncountability of the Reals]\label{cor:reals-uncountable}
$\mathbb{R}$ is uncountable.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Countability of Programs]\label{thm:programs-countable}
The set of all programs in any programming language with a finite alphabet is countable.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Every program is a finite string over a finite alphabet. The set of all finite strings over a finite alphabet is countable (it is a countable union of finite sets). Since programs are a subset of all strings, they are countable.
\end{proof}

\begin{insight}
The countability of programs and the uncountability of functions from $\mathbb{N}$ to $\mathbb{N}$ implies that most functions are uncomputable. This is the fundamental limitation that motivates the study of computability theory (\chref{computability}).
\end{insight}

\section{Applications to Semantics}\label{sec:set-applications}

\subsection{Environments as Functions}

In programming language semantics, an \emph{environment} (or \emph{variable binding}) is a partial function from variable names to values:
\[\rho : \mathrm{Var} \rightharpoonup \mathrm{Val}\]

Environment extension is defined as:
\[\rho[x \mapsto v](y) = \begin{cases} v & \text{if } y = x \\ \rho(y) & \text{if } y \neq x \end{cases}\]

\subsection{States as Functions}

A \emph{state} (or \emph{store}) in the semantics of an imperative language is a function from memory locations to values:
\[\sigma : \mathrm{Loc} \to \mathrm{Val}\]

\subsection{Semantic Functions as Fixed Points}

The meaning of a recursive function or a while loop is defined as a fixed point. Consider:
\[\sembrack{\textbf{while}\;b\;\textbf{do}\;c} = \mathrm{lfp}(F)\]
where $F : (\Sigma \rightharpoonup \Sigma) \to (\Sigma \rightharpoonup \Sigma)$ is defined by:
\[F(f)(\sigma) = \begin{cases} F(f')(\sembrack{c}(\sigma)) & \text{if } \sembrack{b}(\sigma) = \mathbf{tt} \\ \sigma & \text{if } \sembrack{b}(\sigma) = \mathbf{ff} \\ \text{undefined} & \text{if } \sembrack{b}(\sigma) \text{ is undefined} \end{cases}\]

The Knaster--Tarski theorem guarantees that this fixed point exists.

\section{Constructions on Sets}\label{sec:set-constructions}

\subsection{Disjoint Unions}\label{sec:set-disjoint-union}

\begin{definition}[Disjoint Union]\label{def:disjoint-union}
The \emph{disjoint union} (or \emph{coproduct}) of sets $A$ and $B$ is:
\[A + B = (\{0\} \times A) \cup (\{1\} \times B) = \{(0, a) \mid a \in A\} \cup \{(1, b) \mid b \in B\}\]
The injections are $\iota_1(a) = (0, a)$ and $\iota_2(b) = (1, b)$.
\end{definition}

The disjoint union is needed when $A$ and $B$ may overlap: it ensures that elements from $A$ and $B$ are distinguishable even if they are ``the same'' value.

\begin{example}[Tagged Unions in Programming]\label{ex:tagged-union}
In programming, disjoint unions correspond to \emph{tagged unions} (or \emph{variants}, or \emph{sum types}):
\begin{itemize}
  \item In ML: \texttt{type ('a, 'b) either = Left of 'a | Right of 'b}
  \item In Rust: \texttt{enum Either<A, B> \{ Left(A), Right(B) \}}
  \item In Haskell: \texttt{data Either a b = Left a | Right b}
\end{itemize}
\end{example}

\subsection{Function Spaces}\label{sec:set-function-space}

\begin{definition}[Function Space]\label{def:function-space}
The \emph{function space} $B^A$ (also written $A \to B$) is the set of all functions from $A$ to $B$:
\[B^A = \{f \mid f : A \to B\}\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Cardinality of Function Spaces]\label{thm:function-space-card}
If $|A| = m$ and $|B| = n$, then $|B^A| = n^m$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
A function $f : A \to B$ assigns to each of the $m$ elements of $A$ one of the $n$ elements of $B$. There are $n$ choices for each element, and the choices are independent, giving $n^m$ total functions.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}[Boolean Functions]\label{ex:boolean-functions}
The number of functions from $\{0,1\}^n$ to $\{0,1\}$ is $2^{2^n}$. For $n = 2$, there are $2^4 = 16$ Boolean functions of two variables (AND, OR, XOR, implication, etc.).
\end{example}

\subsection{Quotients and Equivalence Relations Revisited}\label{sec:set-quotients-revisited}

\begin{definition}[Canonical Projection]\label{def:canonical-projection}
Given an equivalence relation $\sim$ on $A$, the \emph{canonical projection} is the function $\pi : A \to A/{\sim}$ defined by $\pi(a) = [a]_{\sim}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Universal Property of Quotients]\label{thm:quotient-universal}
A function $f : A/{\sim} \to B$ exists such that $f \circ \pi = g$ (for a given $g : A \to B$) if and only if $g$ respects $\sim$: $a \sim b \implies g(a) = g(b)$. Moreover, $f$ is unique.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $f$ exists, then $a \sim b$ implies $\pi(a) = \pi(b)$, so $g(a) = f(\pi(a)) = f(\pi(b)) = g(b)$. Conversely, if $g$ respects $\sim$, define $f([a]) = g(a)$. This is well-defined because if $[a] = [b]$, then $a \sim b$, so $g(a) = g(b)$. Uniqueness follows because $f$ is determined by $f([a]) = g(a)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}[Type Equality as Quotient]\label{ex:type-quotient}
In a type system with type synonyms (e.g., $\mathrm{type}\;\mathrm{Int} = \mathrm{Integer}$), types are equivalence classes of type expressions under the synonym relation. The canonical projection maps a type expression to its equivalence class.
\end{example}

\section{Relations in Programming Language Theory}\label{sec:set-relations-pl}

\subsection{Reduction Relations}\label{sec:set-reduction}

In operational semantics, the meaning of a program is defined by a \emph{reduction relation}:

\begin{definition}[Reduction Relation]\label{def:reduction-relation}
A \emph{reduction relation} $\longrightarrow$ on a set of terms $T$ is a binary relation $R \subseteq T \times T$. We write $e \longrightarrow e'$ to mean $(e, e') \in R$.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Beta-Reduction]\label{ex:beta-reduction-relation}
In the lambda calculus, beta-reduction is the relation:
\[(\lambda x.\,e_1)\;e_2 \longrightarrow \subst{e_1}{x}{e_2}\]
extended to all contexts (subterms).
\end{example}

\begin{definition}[Confluence]\label{def:confluence-relation}
A reduction relation $\longrightarrow$ is \emph{confluent} if whenever $e \longrightarrow^* e_1$ and $e \longrightarrow^* e_2$, there exists $e_3$ such that $e_1 \longrightarrow^* e_3$ and $e_2 \longrightarrow^* e_3$.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Transition Systems}\label{sec:set-transition}

\begin{definition}[Transition System]\label{def:transition-system}
A \emph{transition system} is a tuple $(S, \to, S_0)$ where:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $S$ is a set of \emph{states}
  \item $\to \;\subseteq S \times S$ is a \emph{transition relation}
  \item $S_0 \subseteq S$ is a set of \emph{initial states}
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

Transition systems are the fundamental model for operational semantics. The states represent configurations of the abstract machine, and the transition relation represents one step of computation.

