\subsection{Checking That a Relation Descends to a Quotient}

A partition of a state space is useful only if a client's observations
can be defined on its blocks. Suppose a client can ask which classes are
reachable by one edge, but cannot see which concrete state it occupies.
The contract is a finite set $S$, a relation $R\subseteq S\times S$ in
adjacency lists, and a total class map $c$ on $S$. The checker either
returns a quotient adjacency map valid for every representative, or
two states in one class whose views disagree. Parallel edges are
irrelevant here: the client observes a set, not a count or an edge
label.

\begin{sudocode}[Check representative independence]
\Function{Quotient}{$S,R,c$}
  \State $M,W\gets$ empty maps \Comment{Views and witnesses}
  \ForAll{$s\in S$}
    \State $k\gets c(s)$; $T\gets\varnothing$
    \ForAll{$t$ in $R[s]$}
      \State Insert $c(t)$ into $T$ \Comment{Forget concrete targets}
    \EndFor
    \If{$k\notin\operatorname{dom}(M)$}
      \State $M[k]\gets T$; $W[k]\gets s$
    \ElsIf{$M[k]\ne T$}
      \State \Return mismatch $(W[k],s)$
    \EndIf
  \EndFor
  \State \Return $M$
\EndFunction
\end{sudocode}

\paragraph{Worked trace.}
Let $c(0)=c(1)=A$, $c(2)=B$, and take edges
$0\to2$, $1\to2$, and $2\to1$. Visiting $0$ stores $\{B\}$ at $A$
and records witness $0$. Visiting $1$ produces the same view and
leaves the entry alone. Visiting $2$ stores $\{A\}$ at $B$. In the
result, a client starting in either concrete member of $A$ can take
one step into $B$. Now add $1\to0$. The view at $1$ becomes
$\{A,B\}$, so the checker returns $(0,1)$. Merely taking the union
of the two views would advertise an $A\to A$ step that cannot be taken
from $0$.

\Cref{fig:visual-3} isolates the added concrete edge that makes the
one-step observation depend on the representative of $A$.

