\paragraph{Invariant and scope.}
After each successful iteration, $M[k]$ equals the view of every
visited state of class $k$. This suffices even for finite sequences of
class observations: each advertised edge can be lifted from the
current concrete representative, so an induction lifts the whole path.
Empty successor sets must be compared too; skipping them would hide
disagreement between a dead end and a live state. The checker is exact
for the given finite graph and observation. It does not justify merging
states for clients that inspect edge multiplicities or other details
omitted from the view.

\section{Exercises}\label{sec:set-exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Prove that the intersection of two equivalence relations is an equivalence relation. Is the union of two equivalence relations necessarily an equivalence relation?
  
  \item Let $R$ be a relation on $A$. Prove that $R^*$ (the reflexive-transitive closure) is the smallest reflexive and transitive relation containing $R$.
  
  \item Draw the Hasse diagram of the powerset $\mathcal{P}(\{a,b,c\})$ ordered by inclusion.
  
  \item Prove that the set of all partial functions from $\mathbb{N}$ to $\mathbb{N}$, ordered by graph inclusion, is a complete partial order but not a complete lattice.
  
  \item Let $f(x) = x^2$ on the lattice $[0,1]$ of real numbers. Find all fixed points and identify the least fixed point.
  
  \item Prove that if $f, g : L \to L$ are both monotone and $f(x) \sqsubseteq g(x)$ for all $x$, then $\mathrm{lfp}(f) \sqsubseteq \mathrm{lfp}(g)$.
  
  \item Show that the set of finite subsets of $\mathbb{N}$ is countable, but the set of all subsets of $\mathbb{N}$ is uncountable.
  
  \item Give an example of a poset that is a lattice but not a complete lattice.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Chapter Summary}\label{sec:set-summary}

We have developed the set-theoretic foundations needed throughout this book:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Sets, functions, and relations provide the basic vocabulary.
  \item Equivalence relations and quotient sets allow us to identify structurally different but semantically equivalent objects (e.g., $\alpha$-equivalence).
  \item Partial orders and lattices provide the framework for reasoning about information content and approximation.
  \item The Knaster--Tarski and Kleene fixed-point theorems guarantee the existence and computability of least fixed points, which underlie the semantics of recursion and iteration.
  \item Cardinality arguments reveal fundamental limitations: most functions are uncomputable.
\end{itemize}

In the next chapter, we deepen our study of order theory and lattices, developing the tools needed for abstract interpretation and program analysis.
